WEBVTT

c0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.000
This is a conversation between Hakham Yosef Faul and Rami Ovadia.

c0002
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000
What's the date?

c0003
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Valentine's Day, February 15th, 2001.

c0004
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
All right.

c0005
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Yes.

c0006
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Interview.

c0007
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Hakham Faul is interviewing Rami Ovadia.

c0008
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Thank you.

c0009
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Have you ever, I'm sure you have, but wondered how languages originate?

c0010
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Nobody knows.

c0011
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.000
This is a very interesting thing.

c0012
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:50.000
In the Académie Française, they have one of the claims is nobody may ask where language or how language developed.

c0013
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.000
It's impossible.

c0014
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.000
It's a given.

c0015
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
And that's why it's a given.

c0016
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Because we never know.

c0017
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:09.000
We never found a human who is non-linguistic or language which is not human.

c0018
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
So when I say human, you're saying language.

c0019
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.000
That's the simple thing of the Targum.

c0020
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Yes.

c0021
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
So that's, nobody knows.

c0022
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Yes.

c0023
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
It's just like nobody knows the origin of neshama.

c0024
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Yes.

c0025
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Except for chimpanzees, mostly professors at the Hebrew University, who since they themselves don't have neshama, they say it's ridiculous.

c0026
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Yes.

c0027
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
It's like if you're going to ask a cat about the origin of feminism.

c0028
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
What do you mean family?

c0029
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Father.

c0030
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.000
What do you mean father?

c0031
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Children.

c0032
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
When did we see that somebody take care of his children?

c0033
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.000
This is ridiculous.

c0034
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Same thing, the chimpanzees do not think that is neshama.

c0035
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Good luck to them.

c0036
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Yes.

c0037
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Interesting thing about Nefesh Hayah.

c0038
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The pasuq says,

c0039
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Speaking of being or whatever it is.

c0040
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Yes.

c0041
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Now, the question is, what is the relationship between Ruach Hayah and Ruach Meir?

c0042
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.000
It is.

c0043
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
What does it mean Nefesh Hayah?

c0044
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Normally we would mistranslate a living soul.

c0045
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.000
No? Nefesh Hayah a living soul?

c0046
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Could be a dead soul?

c0047
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.000
No, dead soul.

c0048
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
We never saw a dead soul.

c0049
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Nefesh Meita.

c0050
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
So what does it mean Nefesh Hayah?

c0051
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Nefesh Hayah means actually Ruach Meir.

c0052
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.000
But for this you have to be intelligent.

c0053
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
And now you could appreciate the sophistication of the Hakhamim.

c0054
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Nefesh Hayah means a Nefesh that is self-conscious, that is conscious of itself.

c0055
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.000
That it knows itself.

c0056
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:23.000
A dog, a chimpanzee, sees the outside universe, but he cannot see himself, cannot look into himself.

c0057
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Human is the only creature that could look into itself.

c0058
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.000
And how can this be done?

c0059
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Through the linguistic apparatus.

c0060
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Because we have a linguistic apparatus, we can look into ourselves.

c0061
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.000
You understand?

c0062
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
And do we have any other case in the Tora where it says Hayah means self-conscious?

c0063
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Yes.

c0064
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
With the Mialedot.

c0065
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Kilo ha'ibriyot ki hayot henna.

c0066
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
They are self-conscious.

c0067
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.000
They know what's going on.

c0068
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.000
You cannot just tell them blah blah blah.

c0069
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Now there's a difference between the ibriyot and the Hayah.

c0070
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Simple.

c0071
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.000
By the way, it's interesting.

c0072
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000
I'm going to show this over a conversation.

c0073
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:26.000
And that Biskut Hashim Sarkanyot nigalot ha'ibriyot ki hayot henna.

c0074
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:36.000
And then we see here these two women are the first case in human record that somebody says no to the king.

c0075
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.000
In other words, the discovery of conscience.

c0076
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.000
There's something above a tyrant.

c0077
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Whether he is para'ot or he is the head of a left-wing party in Israel.

c0078
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Still, there's something above them.

c0079
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:07.000
It's difficult to imagine that something could be more intelligent than some of the great intellectuals flourishing in the gardens of Israel.

c0080
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.000
We humans, we cannot imagine.

c0081
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:16.000
But apparently from the Bible, it seems that there were people who thought that...

c0082
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
All right, yes.

c0083
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:27.000
All right, next question.

c0084
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.000
What is the story of Bereshit with Peter Sartre?

c0085
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Where?

c0086
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:43.000
I know the robe, the robe has something about it.

c0087
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.000
You see, I got it.

c0088
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.000
First of all, you have an excellent, superb book on Bereshit, which is David Cassuto.

c0089
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.000
David Cassuto was a true Tamim Hakham.

c0090
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.000
Some of the things that he says about...

c0091
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:09.000
I found here and there a few little things that contradict things.

c0092
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:17.000
At any point that he contradicts the absolute veracity of the Tora, I don't care whether he's a professor or not.

c0093
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:25.000
But I don't know why sometimes he digresses a little bit and seems to be in a few points.

c0094
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:31.000
But in general, his commentary is really a shot of the Tora.

c0095
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:37.000
And so I would recommend you to look into David Cassuto.

c0096
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Also, he has an excellent book, which I have some place.

c0097
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.000
And I believe that it has been translated into Hebrew.

c0098
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:52.000
It's called, it was in Italian, something about the word Genesis.

c0099
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.000
And it's excellent, excellent, excellent.

c0100
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Incidentally, there were two great biblical scholars.

c0101
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.000
Both of them were Sepharadim.

c0102
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.000
Abraham Shalom Yahudah, Abraham Shalom, the greatest, in my opinion.

c0103
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
And Moshe David Cassuto, the greatest according to many.

c0104
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.000
A third one is Rabbi Yahweh ben Amozed.

c0105
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Three of them Sepharadim.

c0106
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Of course, also Shadal.

c0107
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.000
He's not Sepharadim, he's Italian.

c0108
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.000
But, you know, it's interesting.

c0109
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:37.000
This man, particularly Yahudah and Cassuto, totally, absolutely rejected the whole stupidity of Wellhausen.

c0110
00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.000
You know, what?

c0111
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.000
The biblical criticism.

c0112
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:52.000
And I have to say that those who accepted the theory of Wellhausen, those Jews,

c0113
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
well, first of all, we have to say the truth.

c0114
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.000
They were bad Jews.

c0115
00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.000
They were very poor intellectuals.

c0116
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.000
You know?

c0117
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
They just accepted.

c0118
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.000
You know what happened with those Jews?

c0119
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:08.000
They lived in a dogmatic society.

c0120
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000
And first was the dogma of Judaism.

c0121
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.000
And they rebelled.

c0122
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
And they accepted other dogmas.

c0123
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.000
They had absolutely no critical mind.

c0124
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.000
They used power to impose their ideas.

c0125
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Not persuasion.

c0126
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:32.000
And ultimately there is no difference between, I don't know, this guy, I consider him a terribly mediocre guy, this guy Kaufman, for instance,

c0127
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.000
and a rabbit.

c0128
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:39.000
The only difference is that Kaufman uses different kind of insults.

c0129
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.000
But all this is a question of violence.

c0130
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:49.000
And what characterizes all this tradition, and we have to say it, yes, is that not only are they un-Jewish,

c0131
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:58.000
but they are very vague in the sense that they use violence, yes, to promote the theories.

c0132
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.000
And they were poor Jews.

c0133
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.000
I mean, ultimately, I told you the famous story, you know, about this guy, a great biblical scholar.

c0134
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:12.000
I thought that he was the greatest, a great, in my opinion, a great Semitist.

c0135
00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Yes, he knew Semitic languages.

c0136
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.000
As a lexicographer, he knew the meaning of words very, very nicely.

c0137
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.000
But he couldn't join the two words together.

c0138
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.000
He muttered and he stuttered always.

c0139
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.000
And, you know, I think the stuttering was psychological.

c0140
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Because, yes, at some place I read that stuttering sometimes is due to psychological, not to physiological things.

c0141
00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.000
And this professor, and did I tell you the story about...

c0142
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.000
So, I don't want to say it because it's...

c0143
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.000
No, all right, I said, it's in his book.

c0144
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.000
I respected him, I loved him, and so on.

c0145
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.000
But I tell you the story.

c0146
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.000
The story is, I was, of course, at the JTS, and we always used to discuss philology.

c0147
00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.000
I love languages.

c0148
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:02.000
And I don't know how, once he confessed to me, or he told me something about Berhausen.

c0149
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.000
But for me, he's an idiot.

c0150
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Berhausen was an idiot who was wiser than other idiots who accepted his theory.

c0151
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
And the major thrust of his thing is the Tanakh.

c0152
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.000
The Jewish Tanakh is horrible, which means the only thing we have left is the new one.

c0153
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.000
And, of course, the new one, they will attack.

c0154
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.000
And, of course, the Jews being so stupid.

c0155
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:35.000
I mean, there is no people more stupid than the so-called Jewish intellectuals and left-wing Jewish intellectuals.

c0156
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.000
They are the epitome of stupidity.

c0157
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:43.000
If anybody has a doubt about Hazzad saying that Rashaim, Hayyim, Mikraim, Metim,

c0158
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
should speak with one of the left-wing Jewish intellectuals.

c0159
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.000
Or worse, you know what, you want to know that they are Metim?

c0160
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.000
Speak with the children.

c0161
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.000
Let them know how beautiful parents they are.

c0162
00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.000
Yes? All right.

c0163
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:03.000
Now, I spoke to him, and still I am very fond of him, and I only have good memories,

c0164
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.000
and respect, and even love for him.

c0165
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.000
No question about this.

c0166
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.000
And he was telling me about Berhausen.

c0167
00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.000
So I told him, you know, you told me about Berhausen.

c0168
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Berhausen is based on Hegel.

c0169
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:23.000
Now, modern philosophy, no person, unless you happen to be a Roman Catholic or a pagan,

c0170
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.000
takes Hegel seriously.

c0171
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:31.000
And, I mean, say, Wittgenstein destroyed Hegel.

c0172
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:40.000
So, do you mean to say that if I, because I was educated in the second half of the 20th century,

c0173
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:44.000
and I do not, no longer respect intellectually Hegel,

c0174
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.000
that I cannot be a Jew, an intelligent Jew?

c0175
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.000
Do you know what he has to mean?

c0176
00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Yes.

c0177
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.000
So, Berhausen is, takes the place of Moses.

c0178
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:59.000
These people, they never, never attain true critical knowledge.

c0179
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:07.000
They come from an oppressive, doctrinarian community.

c0180
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:16.000
And I don't know whether the name of the reverie of his grandfather's background,

c0181
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.000
he changed that reverie psychologically for Berhausen.

c0182
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.000
You know, they have the same attitude, I mean, totally uncritical attitude.

c0183
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.000
There's something wrong with these people.

c0184
00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:27.000
Anyhow, so I told him, then I told him,

c0185
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:36.000
would you apply Berhausen's critical theory to the study of the U.S. Constitution?

c0186
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.000
He says, no.

c0187
00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:40.000
And I said, why?

c0188
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:43.000
He says, because I live by the Constitution.

c0189
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.000
And I said, I have news for you.

c0190
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.000
We Jews live by the Tora.

c0191
00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.000
For us, the Tora is the Jewish Constitution.

c0192
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.000
Yes.

c0193
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:58.000
And so, about, we were talking about Bible and so on,

c0194
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.000
the Sephardim, and we have to say it, yes,

c0195
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:05.000
and repeat it a thousand times, yes,

c0196
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.000
the Sephardim did not betray the Bible.

c0197
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:16.000
Those who betrayed the Bible, those intellectuals, left-wing intellectuals,

c0198
00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.000
they did this for Oedipal reasons.

c0199
00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.000
And we have to say that.

c0200
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.000
And I believe that it's even indecent, I don't want to say immoral,

c0201
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:28.000
but I want to use a more powerful word, indecent,

c0202
00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.000
to even discuss their ideas.

c0203
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.000
The people who have no respect for Jewish tradition,

c0204
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.000
who have no respect for Jewish text,

c0205
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.000
and therefore they have nothing to do with Tora, yes,

c0206
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:42.000
let them apply first their theories to other texts, yes,

c0207
00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.000
and then maybe we could have a dialogue, all right?

c0208
00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.000
And again, we have to repeat it a thousand times.

c0209
00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.000
It has nothing to do with being right or wrong.

c0210
00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:55.000
And once I spoke with a student of mine,

c0211
00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:59.000
whom I respect very, very much, Rabbi Yudof,

c0212
00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:05.000
and he told me, well, sometimes you find beautiful things in this.

c0213
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Well, the truth is, I did not find beautiful things in the things.

c0214
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.000
But I told him, Mashal, I'm going to give you a parable,

c0215
00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:19.000
Mashal ne'ma davadome, that I'm going to start excavating

c0216
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:23.000
on 42nd Street and 5th Avenue,

c0217
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:26.000
maybe I'm going to find some interesting mineral, yes?

c0218
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:30.000
Maybe, but meanwhile you're going to destroy the whole city of New York.

c0219
00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.000
It doesn't pay, even if you're going to find something interesting.

c0220
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.000
It simply does not pay.

c0221
00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:40.000
And so anyhow, on this question of the first chapters of Genesis,

c0222
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.000
look into Kasuto, it's terribly intelligent, all right?

c0223
00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:51.000
And since I'm on record, I learned a lot from Kasuto, yes?

c0224
00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.000
Tell you a little bit about my biblical background.

c0225
00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:00.000
Of course, Ibn Ezza, Ibn Janah, you know,

c0226
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.000
mainly Ibn Janah, Ibn Ezza, of course, Radak,

c0227
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.000
no Ramban, no Rashid.

c0228
00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:10.000
Rashid, I love it, but Rashid is the middle name.

c0229
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.000
And, but...

c0230
00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Why no Ramban?

c0231
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:22.000
Because many ideas that Ramban mentions,

c0232
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.000
I found in Christian thought.

c0233
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.000
And it's a flaw, it's a character flaw of mine.

c0234
